            "content": [
              {
                "text": "**Analyzing Shape Interactions**\n\nI'm currently focused on how different shape configurations interact within these grids. I've noted a few key \"training\" examples. For instance, a 2x2 grid of '1's transforms to itself, while a vertical '2' of three elements condenses to its middle element. I'm also examining transformations like two connected components of 0s that are length 2 to all 0s and a singular 0 being size 1.\n\n\n**Investigating Preservation Patterns**\n\nI'm now investigating what determines pixel preservation. Size and neighborhood are key. It looks like the \"central\" pixel is the key feature. My hypothesis is that, at least for some shapes, the central pixel is preserved. The 7 example, as well as the 8 example, support this. However, 1 and 5 seem to suggest that size is also relevant, at least with certain shapes like a square. What is special about the 2x2 shape that it is preserved? Maybe the central property is relevant. Maybe neighborhood?\n\n\n",
                "type": "reasoning_text"
              }
            ],
            "encrypted_content": null,
            "status": "completed",
            "format": "google-gemini-v1"
